  "Number": 30,
  "Question": "\\(A\\) can run around a circular track in \\(40\\) seconds. \\(B,\\) running in the opposite direction, meets \\(A\\) every \\(15\\) seconds. What is \\(B\\)’s time to run around the track, expressed in seconds?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(12\\dfrac12\\)",
    "B": "\\(24\\)",
    "C": "\\(25\\)",
    "D": "\\(27\\dfrac12\\)",
    "E": "\\(55\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Measure distance in laps. Between consecutive meetings, the runners' distances add to exactly one lap because they run in opposite directions.\n\nIn the \\(15\\) seconds between meetings, \\(A\\) runs\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{40}=\\frac38\n\\]\nof a lap. Therefore \\(B\\) runs\n\\[\n1-\\frac38=\\frac58\n\\]\nof a lap in the same time. At this pace, \\(B\\)'s time for one whole lap is\n\\[\n15\\div\\frac58\n=15\\cdot\\frac85\n=24\\text{ seconds}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(24\\) seconds, choice B.",
